WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.650
Every year my second-year students can

2
00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:06.250
recite the formula for a confidence

3
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:06.810
interval.

4
00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.780
Then I ask what the ninety five percent

5
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.700
refers to, and most say: the chance the

6
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.000
true mean is inside this interval.

7
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.080
Today: where a standard error comes from,

8
00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:24.120
how the bootstrap estimates it, and how to

9
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.020
teach both without hand-waving.

10
00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:29.330
An estimate, such as a sample

11
00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.510
mean, is a random variable.

12
00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.340
Its sampling distribution is the

13
00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.320
spread of values it would take over

14
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.920
all possible samples of the same size

15
00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:40.140
from the same population.

16
00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.670
The standard deviation of that

17
00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:46.310
distribution is the standard error.

18
00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.240
For a mean it is sigma over the

19
00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.860
square root of n, so four times the

20
00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.580
data only halves the error.

21
00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:56.490
The central limit theorem says the shape

22
00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:58.730
is close to normal for large n.

23
00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:02.370
We drew a thousand samples of

24
00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.530
thirty exam marks from a skewed

25
00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.630
population and plotted the means.

26
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.520
The marks were skewed, but the

27
00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:11.580
means pile up in a near-normal

28
00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.100
hill around the true mean.

29
00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.650
In real research we have one

30
00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:18.190
sample, not a thousand.

31
00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:21.530
Efron's bootstrap resamples that one

32
00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:24.330
sample with replacement, recomputes the

33
00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:27.310
estimate many times, and uses the spread

34
00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:29.410
of those values as the standard error.

35
00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:32.230
Students meet the formula

36
00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:34.350
first and the idea never.

37
00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:35.670
Reverse that.

38
00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.060
Let them simulate many samples before any

39
00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:42.740
algebra, then derive sigma over root n to

40
00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:44.320
explain what they already saw.

41
00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:45.950
Quick check!

42
00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:49.010
A ninety five percent confidence interval

43
00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:52.350
for a mean is from sixty to sixty eight.

44
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.420
Which statement is correct?

45
00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:58.000
The ninety five percent describes the

46
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.220
method, not this one interval.

47
00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.600
Over many samples, about ninety five

48
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.720
in a hundred intervals built this way

49
00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.540
would contain the true mean.

50
00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.840
In the app, open the sampling lab.

51
00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.060
Draw hundreds of samples, change the

52
00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.380
sample size, and watch the spread of the

53
00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.940
means shrink like one over root n.

54
00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:20.880
Your turn.

55
00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.820
A study has a standard error of four.

56
00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:28.570
How many times more data do we

57
00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:30.530
need to bring it down to two?

58
00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:33.610
Four times as much data.

59
00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:36.930
Remember: an estimate has a sampling

60
00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.510
distribution, its spread is the

61
00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.850
standard error, and the bootstrap

62
00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.670
estimates it from one sample.

63
00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:46.880
Try it in your next lecture: before

64
00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.520
showing any formula, give pairs a

65
00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.240
dataset and ask them to resample it two

66
00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.120
hundred times and plot the means.

67
00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.940
Then ask them to predict what happens

68
00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.960
when n is four times larger.
